---
title: "MÁV 490"
description: "Budapest-built 760 mm 0-8-0T locomotives of MÁV class 490, works numbers 3153–3164 of 1914."
weight: 1
---

## Historical Overview

Marostordai HÉV (*Maros-Torda Helyi Érdekű Vasút*; English: Maros-Torda Local Interest Railway) was a 760 mm narrow-gauge network centred on Târgu Mureș in Transylvania. Its first sections opened in 1915 and it was later operated by MÁV and, after the transfer of Transylvania to Romania, by CFR.

These twelve Budapest-built 760 mm 0-8-0T locomotives were procured by MÁV for its narrow-gauge network. Although they are listed in Pospichal’s Marostordai HÉV survey as locomotives procured by MÁV, their recorded service was largely elsewhere. Only five—490.021, 490.023, 490.026, 490.027 and 490.028—were allocated to CFR after the First World War; the available records do not establish that all five were operated at Târgu Mureș. These locomotives are part of the wider 142-unit MÁV type 70 / class 490 programme built between 1905 and 1950.

The type was originally classified XXIc and redesignated MÁV class 490 after 1911.

## Technical Data

For the 1914 batch, these are the general characteristics for the type 70 family (later class 490), not necessarily as exact specifications of every individual locomotive.

- **Wheel arrangement:** 0-8-0T, with Klien-Lindner hollow axles on the first and fourth coupled axles.  
- **Gauge:** 760 mm.  
- **Driving-wheel diameter:** 750 mm.  
- **Rigid wheelbase:** 1,150 mm; minimum curve radius: 30 m.  
- **Boiler pressure:** 14 bar.  
- **Grate area:** 1.04 m².  
- **Heating surface:** 48.14 m².  
- **Water capacity:** 2 m³.  
- **Power:** about 120 hp / 88.3 kW when wood-fired; the later coal-fired variant developed up to 150 hp / 110 kW.  
- **Speed:** 30 km/h for the coal-fired variant.  
- **Builder:** MÁV Gépgyár (MÁV Machine Factory), Budapest; later MÁVAG.  
- **Works numbers:** 3153–3164 of 1914  
- **MÁV class:** 490  
- **Original MÁV numbers:** 490.020–490.031  

