### Notes on the source

- Pospichal places the movements of 490.023 from Teresva to Gyergyóvárhegy, and of 490.031 from Klausen to the Fleimstalbahn and back to Klausen, within parentheses. They are treated here as separate dated movements.
- The entry `´3.> PDI Ljubija` for 490.025 is interpreted as a move during the 1930s; the exact year is not given in the source.
- The final disposal entry for 490.031, `a 193.`, is incomplete in the source and is therefore rendered as “1930s (?)”.
- Pospichal writes `Gánt JOZSEF`; this is rendered here as **Gánt József**, presumably a locomotive or operator name, pending confirmation from a dedicated Gánt source.

## Glossary

| Term | Description |
|:---|:---|
| MÁV | *Magyar Államvasutak* — Hungarian State Railways. |
| SHS | *Srbi, Hrvati i Slovenci* — Railways of the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, 1918/20–1929. |
| JDŽ | *Jugoslovenske državne železnice* — Yugoslav State Railways, 1929–1941 and again from 1945. |
| HDŽ | *Hrvatske državne železnice* — Croatian State Railways, the railway administration of the Independent State of Croatia, 1941–45. |
| CFR | *Căile Ferate Române* — Romanian State Railways; in locomotive histories, it denotes transfer to, ownership by, or operation under the Romanian state railway. |
| SZD | *Sovetskie zheleznye dorogi* — Soviet Railways, the state railway system of the USSR, 1922–91. |
| Bp | Budapest; MÁV Gépgyár (MÁV Machine Factory), later MÁVAG. |
| kukHV | *Heeresverwaltung* — the Imperial and Royal Military Administration of Austria-Hungary. |
| Krivajatalbahn | The Krivaja Valley Railway, a 760 mm forest railway from Zavidovići through Olovo to Han Pijesak/Kusače in Bosnia, built under Austro-Hungarian rule for timber traffic and closed in 1970. |
| Zabrežje–Valjevo | A 67.4 km, 760 mm narrow-gauge railway in Serbia, opened in 1908 and closed in 1969; it linked the Sava crossing at Zabrežje with Valjevo via Obrenovac and Lajkovac. |
| Gyergyóvárhegy | Hungarian name for Subcetate, Romania; a Transylvanian forestry and industrial railway centre on the Putna Valley (*Putnavölgyi* / *Valea Putnei*) industrial railway. Its 760 mm line to Reczefalva (Romanian: Recea) was opened by the Austro-Hungarian army on 21 November 1916. |
| Rózsahegy | Hungarian name for Ružomberok, Slovakia; in Austro-Hungarian railway records it may refer to the town or its surrounding forestry-railway operations. |
| Bosanski Brod | Today Brod, Bosnia and Herzegovina; a Bosnian terminus on the Sava opposite Slavonski Brod. From 1879 it was the starting point of the 760 mm Bosnian railway to Zenica and, from 1882, Sarajevo. |
| Teresva | Hungarian name for Teresva (Ukrainian: Тересва), Zakarpattia, Ukraine; in First World War railway records it refers to the Tarac Valley local railway, which received loan locomotives from the Austro-Hungarian Military Railway (kukHB). |
| Klausen | German name for Chiusa, South Tyrol, Italy. In First World War locomotive records, kukHV (Klausen) refers to the Austro-Hungarian military administration at Klausen and its 760 mm Klausen–Plan (Val Gardena) railway. |
| Steinbeisbahn | Otto Steinbeis’s 760 mm forestry and public narrow-gauge railway system in north-west Bosnia, principally Prijedor–Srnetica–Drvar–Knin; it operated for timber traffic and closed in 1975. |
| Fleimstalbahn | German name for the Val di Fiemme Railway, a 760 mm Austro-Hungarian military railway from Auer (Ora) to Predazzo in the Dolomites; later an Italian 1,000 mm electric railway, closed in 1963. |
| PDI Ljubija | Industrial railway serving the Ljubija iron-ore mines near Prijedor, Bosnia and Herzegovina; the abbreviation’s exact expansion requires confirmation from the source record. |
| Lillafüred | The Lillafüredi Állami Erdei Vasút (Lillafüred State Forest Railway), a narrow-gauge railway near Miskolc, Hungary, opened in 1920. |
| Gánt | A bauxite-mining and industrial railway location in Hungary; locomotive number 1 and later “Gánt József” refer to locomotives employed there. |
| Taracköz | Locality in the Tarac Valley, Subcarpathia (now Zakarpattia, Ukraine); in the source, K46.911 denotes a locomotive allocation in the Tarac Valley railway area. |
| Marostordai HÉV | *Maros-Torda Helyi Érdekű Vasút* — Maros-Torda Local Interest Railway, a 760 mm narrow-gauge network around Târgu Mureș, Transylvania; opened from 1915 and later operated by MÁV and CFR. |

## Bibliography

- Josef Pospichal, [Lokstatistik: Marostordai HÉV](https://www.pospichal.net/lokstatistik/53812-marostordai%20hev.htm) — immediate source for the locomotive histories, works numbers, allocations and disposal entries.
- Bernhard Schmeiser and Franz Kraus, manuscript records; and Attila Kirchner, database entry 53812 P240517 — underlying sources credited by Pospichal for the Marostordai HÉV locomotive data.
- Sándor Malatinszky, “The Class 490 Steam Engines of MÁV, and the Restoration of Locomotive No. 490,039,” *Periodica Polytechnica Transportation Engineering* 39/1 (2011): 23–30, doi:10.3311/pp.tr.2011-1.05 — class history, technical specifications, wartime and post-Trianon distribution of MÁV type 70 / class 490 locomotives.
